Hello Dr. Broman and Google Doc members!

  I have three problems that I am currently struggling with and would appreciate any help you can offer.  I am working in a plant model with five chromosomes.  I am studying many phenotypes, but am measuring the phenotype four ways: Ambient, Elevated, Diff, Ratio.  For the purposes here, I am only looking at cell density (SD).

The first problem-
I am asking for R to provide me with a list of the markers who have a LOD score of 3 or higher for any of the four phenotype measurements.  My R code and the summary output are provided below:
MY CODE:
SDCore <-read.cross("csv", file="C:/Users/Miranda/SD 167 R Ready.csv", genotypes = c("AA", "AB", "BB"), na.strings = "-", alleles = c("A", "B"))
names(SDCore$pheno)
plot(SDCore, pheno.col=c(1:4))
SDCore <- calc.genoprob(SDCore, step = 1.0, off.end=0.0, error.prob= 1.0e-4, map.function="haldane", stepwidth="fixed")
out.SDCore <- scanone(SDCore, pheno.col=1:4, model="normal", method="em")
out.SDCore.perm <- scanone(SDCore, pheno.col=1:4, model="normal", method="em", n.perm=1000)
summary(out.SDCore, threshold=3, format="allpheno")

OUTPUT:
> summary(out.SDCore, threshold=3, format="allpheno")
         chr pos Ambient Elevated Diff Ratio
c2.loc39   2  39   0.541     2.44 2.01 3.154
c5.loc50   5  50   0.612     3.95 1.09 0.485

My issue is that when I plot the same LOD scores, I find that I have 3 markers that reach the threshold.

 (Picture not uploaded).

This third marker (below in red) does not appear in my summary output initially, but I eventually found it by setting my LOD threshold to 1 instead of 3.

Marker	Chromosome	Position	Ambient	Elevated	Diff	Ratio
c2.loc39	2	39	0.541	2.44	2.01	3.154
c5.loc50	5	50	0.612	3.95	1.09	0.485
**MSAAT5.12	5	71.6	1.2931	3.212	0.4	0.234**

Perhaps the marker wasn’t reported because it falls within the support interval of the neighboring peak?  That is my best guess right now, but would appreciate any input!

